7.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: {F. None of the above}

The sustained focus on the orphan line at 435 is the most visible textual detail, yet it does not automatically isolate countour once the poem’s larger metrical and phonological habits are weighed. The three primary manuscripts transmit the line, and the surrounding couplet architecture—especially the documented rime riche precedent at 397–398—shows Chaucer already comfortable with identical rhymes; Thynne’s supplement restores a second countour in precisely that established pattern, which is why Skeat and later editors retained it.  

At the same time, the vowel-quality objection raised for fool and hool continues to merit attention. Under the historical distinction between /oː/ and /ɔː/, the early date of the Book of the Duchess already shows Chaucer exploiting latitude in other -ol/-ool and -ere/-ere pairings (notably in the Ceyx/Alcyone section). That flexibility keeps both words inside the acceptable rhyme range rather than marking either as an outlier, but it also prevents us from treating every listed item as categorically rhymed without residual uncertainty. With the consistent octosyllabic scheme and the documented tolerance for near-identical rhymes therefore accounting for the full set of candidates, F remains the reading that best fits the complete evidence rather than privileging a single contested lacuna.}

